    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 55 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a structured address form for owner creation while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible.\n\n**`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` string properties to the `OwnerFields` schema (so they're accepted on input and returned on output), removed `address` from the `required` list, and documented `address` as the composed output value.\n\n**`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added persisted `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields with normalizing setters (same normalization as the flat address: trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand `ST\u2192STREET` etc.). Rewrote `getAddress()` to prefer the structured form: when `addressLine1` is non-blank it returns the normalized line 1, appending `\" \" + addressLine2` when line 2 is present; otherwise it falls back to the flat `address`. The `@NotEmpty` DB-level guard on `address` stays satisfied because the controller writes the composed value back.\n\n**`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Replaced the flat-address validation with a check on the effective (composed) address after mapping: an owner is rejected (400) only when *neither* a non-blank `addressLine1` nor a flat `address` is supplied. The composed value is written back into the flat field so the persisted `address`, the household/postcode/locality logic (which read via `getAddress()` and the direct `city`/`postcode` fields), and the returned DTO all stay consistent. Missing `city` is still rejected by bean validation.\n\n**Schema SQL** (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres) \u2014 Added nullable `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to the `owners` table.\n\nThe three checkpoint tests exercising this (Cp01 required-fields, Cp12 normalization, Cp44 structured accept + compose) and the full existing suite are green.",
